  "version": 2,
  "meta": {
    "id": "gvm-watchdog-architecture",
    "title": "GVM Watchdog Architecture",
    "subtitle": "Kernel petting, virtual bark / bite and managed GVM restart",
    "description": "The QNX host executes qvm, vmm_service and vmm_lifecycle. Android kernel modules pet a trapped watchdog register. qvm loads vdev-msm.so, emulates the guest watchdog with guest_timer, injects bark, handles bite and reports event 3 through /dev/vmm. In the configured manual mode, vmm_service performs ordered client cleanup, waits for qvm termination and sends GVM_EVENT_DOWN to vmm_lifecycle. vmm_lifecycle requests START, and vmm_service spawns qvm. QNX is the host OS, not a Gunyah guest; the Hypervisor band spans only the Android guest. The independent physical APSS watchdog and debug host-ramdump override are outside this focused diagram. Kernel build configuration declares pet 6000 ms, bark 30000 ms and bite bark plus 3000 ms; these are not measurements of the supplied runtime log.",
    "scope": "subsystem",
    "view": "layered",
    "colorMode": "technical"
  },
